# Clean Architecture Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines enforce strict Clean Architecture principles but may be adapted for edge cases when necessary. The core dependency flow must always point inward: presentation → domain ← data.

## Best Practices
- Keep all dependencies pointing inward (outer layers depend on inner layers)
- Ensure Domain layer is independent of any external frameworks or libraries
- Use abstractions (interfaces) for all repositories in the Domain layer
- Implement Use Cases for each business operation
- Keep entities pure and free of UI or database concerns
- Use mappers to convert between domain entities and data models

## File / Project Structure Guidelines
- Maintain clear layer separation:
  - `lib/domain/`: Entities, repositories (interfaces), use cases, value objects
  - `lib/data/`: Repository implementations, data sources, models, mappers
  - `lib/presentation/`: UI components, screens, widgets, state management
  - `lib/core/`: Shared utilities, constants, services
- Restrict imports to follow the dependency rule:
  - Domain should not import from data or presentation
  - Data can import from domain but not presentation
  - Presentation can import from domain and data

## Coding & Styling Conventions
- Name repository interfaces without suffix: `MessageRepository`
- Name implementations with `Impl` suffix: `MessageRepositoryImpl`
- Name use cases as imperative actions: `SendMessageUseCase`
- Prefer composition over inheritance for reuse
- Use abstract classes for interfaces (not Dart's `abstract interface class`)
- Mark domain entities as `@immutable` or make them immutable by design

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Keep domain entities lightweight
- Avoid deep object hierarchies that may cause serialization bottlenecks
- Cache repository results when appropriate
- Consider using value objects for validated primitives
- Perform heavy operations in the data layer, not in the domain layer

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Unit test each layer independently
- Mock dependencies at layer boundaries
- Test domain use cases without any dependencies on external frameworks
- Test repository implementations with mocked data sources
- Use Test-Driven Development (TDD) when implementing new features
